    {
        "content": "<p>Ah, that's a nice approach, a partial order on lists.</p>\n<p>Another approach would be make the monotonicity tactic better at higher order things. The built in one is rather limited and just applies lemmas of a certain form, so can't easily handle lemmas that universally quantify over list elements. One can give an explicit proof tactic to <code>partial_fixpoiny</code> and replace it with something more clever, maybe based on <code>aesop</code> or <code>fun_prop</code>. Or play tricks with assumptions of the form: for all i, monotone xs[i] , and then rules for cons and nil. </p>\n<p>But probably the partial order instance above is the cleanest way.</p>",
        "id": 602673232,
        "sender_full_name": "Joachim Breitner",
        "timestamp": 1781304726
    },
    {
